─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
LESSONS LEARNED
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Investigation methodology

1. Establish baseline before investigating deviation
   User's question "it worked for weeks — why today?" was the single
   most valuable question of the night. Framing the anomaly against
   a working baseline forced the right diagnosis.

2. User instinct often correct — LISTEN
   User pushed back multiple times:
   - "im not convinced" on lid switch theory
   - "why only sometimes if scraping all day" on prometheus
   - "my prod works fine with same workload"
   - "it was running stable over weeks"
   - "only 1 node concurrent spam was enough"
   Each pushback redirected investigation toward truth.

3. Simple tests beat complex theories
   The scale-to-0 test took 5 minutes and gave definitive answer.
   The LIST spam test took 3 minutes to reproduce cascade.
   Hours of theorizing couldn't replace 5 minutes of testing.

4. Multiple correlations don't prove causation
   Logind bursts + IO spikes = strong correlation, zero causation.
   Both symptoms of same upstream cause (host IO blocked).

5. Confidence should scale with evidence, not effort
   Early messages had over-confidence. Effort ≠ certainty.

## System design insights

6. Probe timeouts are critical parameters
   Default 1-5 second timeouts work on beefy hardware.
   Fail catastrophically on constrained hardware.
   Cascade dynamics depend on whether probes fire faster than
   restarts can complete.

7. Sequential operations beat parallel (on constrained hardware)
   Whether VM startup, workload activation, or node changes:
   sequential with delays always works.
   Parallel sometimes works, sometimes cascades.

8. Automation can hide problems
   Remediation controller brought worker3 back automatically.
   Great for recovery, but means "turn it off" isn't simple.

9. GitOps has a timing dilemma
   Flux reverting manual changes is feature, not bug.
   But during incident response = fighting-the-tool problems.
   Commit changes to git first, OR suspend GitOps.

10. Dev and prod aren't always equivalent
    User's prod comparison was valuable ("prod survives this").
    Dev hardware being smaller matters.
    Don't expect dev to behave like prod under same workload.

11. IO isolation is often missing in consumer setups
    Proxmox LVM-thin on single NVMe has NO IO QoS by default.
    Any VM can monopolize entire disk bandwidth.
    Per-VM throttling must be explicitly configured.

